/home/lixinyu/snap/a/key_combo/code/opus/5/002/codegen_logs/8.full.log:6107:1275298       02:05  0.0  0.0 node /home/lixinyu/.nvm/versions/node/v18.20.8/bin/codex exec --skip-git-repo-check -c model="gpt-5.4" -c reasoning.effort="high" 请严格执行下面的 ARC 新题代码生成提示词。必须把代码写入 prompt 指定的目标路径，并运行 prompt 里的自检命令。生成代码前必须把 `/home/lixinyu/snap/new/solvers/arc_utils.py` 作为公共参考工具箱快速阅读，优先复用其中的坐标、bbox、连通域、裁剪、变换和颜色等通用实现；若直接 import，必须保证目标文件可被 `SourceFileLoader` 直接加载。  执行每个代码生成 prompt 时，还必须遵守以下语义一致性硬约束：  1. 输出必须唯一可判定    输入中的线索必须足以唯一确定目标对象、目标操作和结果。    不允许依赖含糊解释，或让同一可见线索在不同语境下产生不同答案。    注意不可多个不同样例的输入网格处理后的输出网格都相同  2. 组合步骤必须不可替代    题目描述中的关键步骤都应对结果产生必要影响。    不能让某一步只是表面存在，实际可被更简单的捷径完全替代。  3. 实现必须遵守题面定义的参照系    所有判断、匹配、距离、对称、排序、分区等，都必须使用题面指定的对象范围和坐标参照。    不得为了实现方便换成另一套参照系。  4. 生成样例要覆盖反例    生成器要主动产生能排除错误捷径的样例。    不只生成最容易通过的标准形态，还要覆盖边界情况、分支情况和易混淆情况。  5. 验证要检查语义而不只是自洽    不能只验证 main(generate_input()) 能闭环。    还要检查实现是否真的满足题面规则、关键依赖、唯一性和非退化性。  你要为一个新的 ARC-AGI 风格题目实现代码。代码文件必须包含 `main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray` 和 `generate_input() -> np.ndarray`。  ## 新题位置  - 描述目录：`/home/lixinyu/snap/a/key_combo/des/opus/5` - 题号：`7` - 目标代码路径：`/home/lixinyu/snap/a/key_combo/code/opus/5/012/7.py`  ## 新题描述  <new_puzzle index="7"> ## 新题 7：嵌入标记选择规则示例并按连接方向印章  **组合逻辑**：[嵌入标记像素(embedded_marker) + 颜色配对(color_pairing)] → 选择规则(创新：标记色与规则示例的源色匹配来选择规则) → [印章放置(stamp) + 模板图案(stencil_pattern) + 颜色替换(color_substitution)] + [标记侧面决定方向(marker_side_dir)](创新：嵌入标记在精灵体内的相对位置决定印章方向)  **来源原语**：嵌入标记像素、多色精灵体、颜色配对（54dc2872）；规则示例、模板图案、颜色替换、示例定义规则（abc82100）；标记侧面决定方向（e9fc42f2）  **依赖链**：精灵体的嵌入标记颜色与规则示例的源色匹配→选定对应的模板图案和目标色→嵌入标记在精灵体边界框中的相对位置（上/下/左/右侧）决定模板图案的旋转角度→模板图案以标记位置为锚点、旋转后印章到精灵体上并重涂为目标色。颜色配对、方向判断、印章放置三步紧密耦合。  每个多色精灵体中的嵌入标记颜色决定适用哪条规则示例，标记在精灵体中的位置决定模板图案的旋转方向，旋转后的模板以目标颜色印章到标记位置。  **输入结构**：约 15x15 的网格。背景为黑(0)。网格中有 2~3 个多色精灵体，每个精灵体有一种主体色（红(2) 或蓝(1)，4~8 像素，4-连通，形状不规则）和一个嵌入标记像素（绿(3) 或黄(4)），标记像素位于精灵体边界框的某条边上（明确在上边、下边、左边或右边）。网格角落有 2 个规则示例区域，每个由一个源色像素与一个目标色像素 4-连通相邻组成，其延伸方向指向一个 2x2 或 2x3 的青(8) 模板图案（图案距源-目标像素对 1~2 格，沿延伸方向排列）。源色与嵌入标记色相同（绿(3) 或黄(4)），目标色为橙(7) 或品红(6)。精灵体之间、精灵体与规则示例之间互不相邻。生成约束：每种嵌入标记色恰好对应一条规则示例。  **规则**： 1. 识别规则示例：找到由源色像素和目标色像素组成的相邻对（源色为绿(3) 或黄(4)，目标色为非背景非源色的其他颜色），以及沿其延伸方向最近的青(8) 连通分量作为模板图案。记录每条规则的源色、目标色和模板图案形状。 2. 对每个精灵体，找到其中唯一的嵌入标记像素（颜色不同于主体色的像素）。 3. 将嵌入标记颜色与规则示例的源色匹配，选定该精灵体适用的规则。 4. 判断嵌入标记在精灵体边界框中的位置：若标记在边界框最上行则方向为"上"，最下行为"下"，最左列为"左"，最右列为"右"（若标记同时在角落，取行优先即先判断上/下）。 5. 模板图案默认朝向为"上"（规则示例中的原始方向）。根据精灵体标记方向旋转模板：上→不旋转，右→顺时针 90 度，下→180 度，左→逆时针 90 度。 6. 将旋转后的模板图案以嵌入标记位置为锚点（对齐到模板的左上角）印章绘制，颜色替换为规则的目标色。印章覆盖背景和已有精灵体像素。 7. 擦除所有规则示例区域（源像素、目标像素、青色模板）和所有嵌入标记像素（标记位置恢复为主体色）。  **关键洞察**：规则的选择和模板的旋转方向都编码在精灵体的局部结构中——嵌入标记的颜色是选择信号，其位置是方向信号。必须同时解码颜色和空间位置两层信息，才能正确完成印章放置。  --- </new_puzzle>  ## 机制相似参考题  下面参考题由本地相似度流程自动选择：优先使用新题中已经写明的原语、组合逻辑、来源原语和显式参考题 id；再用 `annotations`/docstring/代码特征抽取机制标签，按机制相似度、文本相似度和代码特征相似度重排，并用 MMR 做去重。优先学习这些题的对象识别方式、随机生成器约束、边界条件处理，不要照搬旧题规则。  已选参考：eval/21897d95, train/f0100645, train/465b7d93, train/337b420f, train/df978a02, eval/a251c730, train/54dc2872, train/e9fc42f2  新题正文显式提及的参考题 id：1b8318e3, 20fb2937, 21897d95, 337b420f, 465b7d93, 50c07299, 54dc2872, 60d73be6, 758abdf0, 83eb0a57, 84ba50d3, 8abad3cf, 984d8a3e, 9caf5b84, a251c730, abc82100, df978a02, e9fc42f2, f0100645, f931b4a8  文件头参考题组 id（弱提示）：none  <reference_puzzle rank="1" source="eval" puzzle_id="21897d95"> <similarity> score=1.0934 mechanism=1.0000 lexical=0.0168 code_feature=0.7500 overlap_tags=none </similarity> <paths> code=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/21897d95.py annotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/21897d95.json </paths> <description> Repaint regions from T-shaped control markers, erase the markers, and optionally rotate based on a color-9 L-marker.  Input structure: - The grid is a mosaic of monochrome regions. - Color 1 forms T-shaped control markers on region boundaries. - Some controls use three singleton color-1 pixels around a colored center; that center is a literal replacement color. - An optional 5-cell L-shaped color-9 marker triggers 90-degree rotation of the output.  Rule: - For a color-1 T marker, the single stem points at the destination region to repaint. - The two diagonal cells behind the stem identify the source region; the destination gets that source region's original color. - For a colored-center T marker, the same stem identifies the destination, but the center color is the replacement. - All marker cells are filled with the final color of the source-side region. - A 5-cell L-shaped color-9 marker controls rotation: ┐ (corner top-right) → CCW 90°, ┌ (corner top-left) → CW 90°.  Key insight: - The stem chooses what changes, while the two diagonals behind the stem choose where the marker itself belongs. </description> <annotation> {   "type": "transform_in_place",   "src": "monochrome regions with T-shaped color-1 control markers",   "transform": "recolor the region pointed to by each T stem using the original color from the two diagonal source cells behind the stem; colored-center T markers use the center color instead; erase marker cells into the source-side region",   "grid_shape": "same, except rotate 90 degrees when indicated by the colored-center control orientation",   "notes": "small non-rectangular colored scraps are absorbed into the neighboring region" } </annotation> </reference_puzzle>  <reference_puzzle rank="2" source="train" puzzle_id="f0100645"> <similarity> score=1.0702 mechanism=1.0000 lexical=0.0083 code_feature=0.5714 overlap_tags=none </similarity> <paths> code=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/f0100645.py annotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/f0100645.json </paths> <description> Slide each inner colored object horizontally into the same-colored vertical wall until it touches an obstacle.  Input structure - The background is the dominant color. - The only foreground colors are two non-background colors. - One foreground color fills the left column and the other fills the right column. - Extra 8-connected objects of each wall color appear away from that color's wall.  Rule - Clear the extra objects from their original positions. - For each wall color, process its 8-connected extra objects from nearest to farthest from the matching wall. - Slide each object horizontally toward its matching wall as far as possible without overlapping the wall or an already settled object of the same color. - Keep the two vertical wall columns fixed.  Key insight - The side columns act like color-specific gravity walls: same-colored objects compact sideways against their own wall while preserving each object's shape. </description> <annotation> {   "type": "move_along_path",   "src": "8-connected objects away from the full-height side wall of the same color",   "path": "horizontal slide toward the matching side wall, stopping just before the wall or an already settled same-color object would overlap",   "trajectory": "translate each object rigidly to its final unobstructed position",   "transform": "clear original object positions, keep both side walls fixed, and preserve each moved object's color and shape",   "multi_step": "For each wall color, settle objects from nearest to farthest from that wall so later objects can stop against earlier same-color objects." } </annotation> </reference_puzzle>  <reference_puzzle rank="3" source="train" puzzle_id="465b7d93"> <similarity> score=1.0646 mechanism=1.0000 lexical=0.0229 code_feature=0.5000 overlap_tags=none </similarity> <paths> code=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/465b7d93.py annotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/465b7d93.json </paths> <description> Move the small colored sprite into the hollow frame by stretching its shape to the frame interior.  Input structure: - The background is the dominant color. - There are exactly two foreground colors: one forms a complete rectangular   one-pixel-thick frame, and the other forms one 4-connected sprite outside it. - The frame interior is initially background.  Rule: - Clear the original sprite from its outside location. - Map the sprite bbox to the frame interior bbox, sending bbox extremes to   interior extremes. - Draw straight horizontal or vertical segments between mapped positions of   adjacent sprite pixels, then fill any enclosed background region inside   those drawn segments.  Key insight: The outside sprite is a miniature line/fill diagram for the frame interior, not a bitmap to scale by block replication. </description> <annotation> {   "type": "move_to_match",   "src": "small colored sprite s outside the frame",   "dst": "hollow rectangular frame interior d",   "match_cond": "unique s maps to unique d; s.bbox extremes align to d.bbox extremes",   "transform": "clear s; stretch s into d by mapping bbox extremes, drawing 4-adjacent segments, and filling enclosed regions" } </annotation> </reference_puzzle>  <reference_puzzle rank="4" source="train" puzzle_id="337b420f"> <similarity> score=1.0550 mechanism=1.0000 lexical=0.0180 code_feature=0.4286 overlap_tags=none </similarity> <paths> code=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/337b420f.py annotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/337b420f.json </paths> <description> Keep the larger sprite from each separated panel and overlay those sprites into one 5x5 canvas.  Input structure: - The input is a 5x17 grid: three 5x5 panels separated by one-cell black columns. - Each panel has the same background color and exactly one foreground color split into two 4-connected components.  Rule: - In each panel, discard the smaller foreground component and keep the unique largest component as the sprite. - Process panels left to right, placing each sprite at its panel coordinates on a 5x5 background canvas. - If a sprite would cover an already placed sprite, slide it by the unique nearest in-bounds offset that avoids overlap.  Key insight: - The extra same-colored component in each panel is a distractor; the final answer is the non-overlapping overlay of the three largest components. </description> <annotation> {   "type": "move_in_order",   "src": "largest 4-connected foreground object in each 5x5 panel",   "dst": "overlay onto one 5x5 canvas in left-to-right panel order",   "transform": "discard the smaller same-color object from each panel; when a later object overlaps earlier objects, slide it by the nearest in-bounds non-overlapping offset",   "grid_shape": "5x17 input with separator columns -> 5x5 output" } </annotation> </reference_puzzle>  <reference_puzzle rank="5" source="train" puzzle_id="df978a02"> <similarity> score=1.0533 mechanism=1.0000 lexical=0.0093 code_feature=0.4286 overlap_tags=none </similarity> <paths> code=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/df978a02.py annotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/df978a02.json </paths> <description> Let the largest center-pointing object grow outward while every other object loses its inward tip.  Input structure - The grid has a single dominant background color and four 8-connected monochromatic foreground objects. - The objects surround a central background gap and point toward it from cardinal directions. - Exactly one object has the largest area.  Rule - Find the central background gap by minimizing aggregate distance to the four objects. - Recolor the nearest-to-center pixel of each non-largest object to background. - Add a three-pixel cap in the largest object's color on the side away from the center, centered on the shared row or column.  Key insight - The edit is a transfer of prominence around a common center: losers retract at their tips, and the unique largest object extends outward from the center. </description> <annotation> {   "type": "transform_in_place",   "src": "four 8-connected monochromatic objects pointing toward a shared central bg pixel",   "transform": "identify the unique largest object; recolor each other object's nearest-to-center tip pixel to bg and add a 3-pixel cap in the largest object's color on its side away from the center" } </annotation> </reference_puzzle>  <reference_puzzle rank="6" source="eval" puzzle_id="a251c730"> <similarity> score=1.0640 mechanism=1.0000 lexical=0.0200 code_feature=0.5000 overlap_tags=none </similarity> <paths> code=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/a251c730.py annotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/a251c730.json </paths> <description> Stamp source sprites into a destination frame via color-matched singleton markers.  Input structure: - Two bordered rectangles: source (multi-cell sprites) and destination (singleton markers). - Each sprite has an anchor pixel — its least-frequent color. Markers share these colors.  Rule: - Match each destination marker to the source sprite with the same anchor color. - Translate the sprite so its anchor overlaps the marker position. - Output is the destination rectangle, interior cleared before stamping.  Key insight: The rarest color per sprite doubles as a template key and positioning anchor. </description> <annotation> {   "type": "move_to_match",   "src": "sprite s with anchor pixel inside the source framed rectangle",   "dst": "anchored on singleton marker pixel d inside the destination framed rectangle",   "match_cond": "s.anchor_color == d.color",   "transform": "overlap s.anchor with d",   "grid_shape": "crop to only retain the destination rectangle" } </annotation> </reference_puzzle>  <reference_puzzle rank="7" source="train" puzzle_id="54dc2872"> <similarity> score=1.0618 mechanism=1.0000 lexical=0.0090 code_feature=0.5000 overlap_tags=none </similarity> <paths> code=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/54dc2872.py annotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/54dc2872.json </paths> <description> Move each marked L-shaped sprite onto the separate pixel matching its marker color.  Input structure - The background is the dominant color. - Foreground has 4-connected multicolor sprites and isolated one-pixel controls. - Each sprite has one repeated body color in an L shape plus one embedded marker   pixel of a different color.  Rule - If the embedded marker color also appears as a separate isolated control pixel,   translate the whole sprite so the body L-bend lands on that control pixel. - Clear each moved sprite from its old position, clear its consumed control pixel,   then paste all translated sprites; sprites without a matching control stay put.  Key insight - The marker color pairs a sprite with its destination control, while the L-bend   gives the orientation-independent anchor cell to place on that destination. </description> <annotation> {   "type": "move_to_match",   "src": "marked L-shaped sprite s with one embedded marker pixel",   "dst": "isolated control pixel d",   "match_cond": "s.marker.color == d.color and d lies on the outer diagonal ray from s.L_bend through s.marker",   "transform": "translate s so s.L_bend lands on d; clear original s and consumed d; keep unmatched sprites and unrelated single pixels unchanged" } </annotation> </reference_puzzle>  <reference_puzzle rank="8" source="train" puzzle_id="e9fc42f2"> <similarity> score=1.0627 mechanism=1.0000 lexical=0.0135 code_feature=0.5000 overlap_tags=none </similarity> <paths> code=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/e9fc42f2.py annotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/e9fc42f2.json </paths> <description> Assemble the foreground objects by docking equal-colored boundary markers edge-to-edge.  Input structure The grid has a dominant background color and several 4-connected multicolor foreground objects.  One foreground color is the body color; the less frequent foreground colors are boundary connector markers, and each marker color appears once as a free end or twice as a matched docking pair.  Rule Crop each foreground object to its bounding box and translate the objects so that the two occurrences of every paired connector color touch on their exposed boundary sides.  Preserve each object's orientation and colors, then return the tight crop around the assembled foreground on the original background color.  Key insight The colored marker pixels are ports: matching colors specify which objects must touch, and the marker's side of its object determines the d